{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "polarity",
      "components": ["T3"],
      "pins": ["T3.2", "T3.3"],
      "nets": ["/+24V_F", "+24V"],
      "observed": "T3 (AON6407, P-channel MOSFET) has its source (pin 2) connected to /+24V_F (the fused input from the 24V supply) and its drain (pin 3) connected to +24V (the main board rail).",
      "expected": "For a P-channel MOSFET used as high-side reverse-polarity protection, the drain should be on the input side and the source on the output side, so that the body diode is reverse-biased under reverse input polarity and blocks current.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"6\") (name \"/+24V_{F}\")\n      (node (ref \"T3\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"S\") (pintype \"power_in\")) ... )"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"3\") (name \"+24V\")\n      (node (ref \"T3\") (pin \"3\") (pinfunction \"D\") (pintype \"power_out\")) ... )"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"T3\")\n      (value \"AON6407\")\n      (description \"P-Channel 30 V 32A...\")"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "Reverse polarity protection is defeated. If the 24V input is connected backwards, T3's body diode (anode at drain, cathode at source) becomes forward-biased, conducting current from the +24V rail to the reversed input and exposing the regulator, drivers, and other components to reverse voltage.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain an anomaly: the P-channel reverse-polarity protection MOSFET T3 has its source and drain swapped, so it will not block a reversed 24V input and could allow damaging reverse current into the board."
}

